  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "SPOT_UP 2.83 PPP (100% makes) and TRANSITION 0.62 PPP because assisted flag exists only on makes; after rewrite SPOT_UP ~1.16, TRANSITION ~1.20."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "FAIL decay-sanity: DRIVE lifts +0.015,+0.020,+0.018,+0.021,+0.020 (flat). Within-team: DRIVE lag-1 +0.015 to -0.002. Team-quality confound."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
   "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
   "finding": "Inferred label cannot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently; near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
   "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
   "finding": "PUTBACK is top suppressor for 6+ stars (e.g. D. Mitchell 1.178 to 0.884, d=-0.294, n_after=95; B. Beal d=-0.345 n=32; C. Holmgren d=-0.222 n=52). Downstream effects are small and lack multiplicity correction."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
   "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
   "finding": "Confident label rates 14.9% and 3.3% for on-ball defensive schemes; NBA matchups feed did not populate switches_on."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-030",
   "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
   "finding": "Backfilled play-type labels failed to match expected league distributions and team identities (e.g., PNR 28.7% FAIL vs 20-28% band, PULL_UP 7.9% FAIL, 2024-25 PULL_UP ~0-1% for many teams). 82.8% of PNR labels proxy_calibrated."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-001",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "Holdout games where a team's play-type mix is closer to the model's recommended mix score more efficiently. Strength-controlled gap +3.27 ORtg (+0.0193 vs -0.0134 PPP residual)."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-075",
   "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
   "finding": "4/8 PTs downstream real: CUT Ext 1.658 (supp +0.0220), TRANSITION 1.509 (+0.0145), SPOT_UP 1.303 (+0.0124), POST_UP 1.127 (+0.0194). DRIVE downstream not real (opp ATT +0.0024). Downstream effects are small (~0.01-0.02 PPP) relative to direct PPP gaps."
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-014",
   "what_failed": "Auditing play-type label accuracy",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw), LIM:label_or_classification_error, LIM:leakage, LIM:small_sample"
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-096",
   "what_failed": "Assuming possession-level play types from PBP contain unbiased predictive structure",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)"
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
   "what_failed": "Testing for decaying cross-possession memory (lag-1 effect >= lag-2)",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw), LIM:confounding"
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
   "what_failed": "Inferring defensive scheme changes from play-type labels",
   "why": "A_unanswerable (question not answerable with the data or design), LIM:label_or_classification_error"
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
   "what_failed": "Labeling on-ball defensive coverage (DROP/ICE/SWITCH) using NBA matchup/PBP fields",
   "why": "A_unanswerable (question not answerable with the data or design), LIM:label_or_classification_error, LIM:small_sample"
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-030",
   "what_failed": "Verifying backfilled play-type labels against expected distributions and team identities",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw), LIM:label_or_classification_error"
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
   "what_failed": "Assuming play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "There are persistent and significant issues with the accuracy, distribution, and consistency of play-type labels across seasons, including recent ones (2024-25). These issues lead to artifacts in PPP calculations and confound analyses.",
  "Apparent cross-possession (lagged) effects of play types can be artifacts of confounding factors like team quality, collapsing when controlled for.",
  "Measuring small downstream effects (e.g., ~0.01-0.02 PPP) requires robust methodology and multiplicity correction, as individual findings can be statistically weak or unreliable due to small sample sizes for specific sequences.",
  "It is difficult to infer real defensive adjustments or underlying causal links from play-type labels due to label noise and inability to separate offensive execution from defensive response."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "Whether, with corrected and reliable play-type labels, opponent next-possession PPP after a given play type is large enough to drive 'AVOID' labels.",
  "The true magnitude and decay of 'own-sequence lags' effects once label quality and confounding are adequately addressed.",
  "A definitive methodology for identifying or defining 'AVOID' labels based on lagged effects.",
  "Whether the specific issues with play-type labels have been fully resolved in the most recent data."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "The fundamental premise of the research question — analyzing play-type effectiveness based on lagged PPP outcomes — is severely undermined by the documented unreliability of the play-type labels themselves.",
  "The 'AVOID' label concept is premature given the data quality issues and the difficulty in establishing reliable lagged effects.",
  "Even if label quality were perfect, controlling for confounding factors in sequential basketball data (e.g., score differential, game state, personnel on court, team strength) is complex and has led to artifactual findings in the past."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The extent to which the identified play-type label issues (FAM:SF-APPM-014, FAM:SF-PAP-096, FAM:SF-APPA-030, FAM:SF-CHT-025) persist in the entirety of the 2025-26 data or have been fully addressed by engineering efforts not reflected in these records.",
  "The precise definition and criteria for 'AVOID' labels as envisioned by the research question.",
  "The specific statistical power achievable for detecting small lagged effects across all play types and team combinations, even with clean data."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes",
  "explanation": "The historical evidence profoundly changed the decision from potentially proceeding to not proceeding. Before retrieval, the question seemed plausible, assuming reasonable data quality. The retrieved records (FAM:SF-APPM-014, FAM:SF-PAP-096, FAM:SF-APPA-030, FAM:SF-CHT-025) reveal systemic and persistent issues with play-type label accuracy, distribution, and artifact generation, making any analysis built on these labels unreliable. Furthermore, FAM:SF-CHT-029 demonstrated that apparent lagged effects could be artifacts of confounding, directly challenging the 'lags similarly matter' aspect. FAM:SF-APPS-075 showed that even 'real' downstream effects are small and require careful handling. This collective evidence indicates that the foundational data quality and methodological challenges are currently insurmountable for this question."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.05,
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
    "text": "The core input, play-type labels, have been repeatedly shown to be unreliable, inaccurate, and prone to artifacts (e.g., foul leakage, skewed PPP values, non-representative distributions). This directly undermines any analysis of their effectiveness or lagged impact."
   },
   {
    "code": "CONFOUNDING",
    "text": "Prior attempts to identify lagged effects have been confounded by factors like team quality, leading to artifactual findings. The complex sequential nature of basketball possessions makes it extremely difficult to isolate the causal effect of a single play type on future possessions without robust controls for game state, personnel, and opponent quality."
   },
   {
    "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
    "text": "While overall possession counts are large, detecting small lagged effects for specific play types, especially when considering team-level or player-level interactions, can lead to small effective sample sizes and low statistical power, as seen in FAM:SF-APPA-050 and FAM:SF-APPS-075."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "partly",
  "cited": [
   "FAM:SF-APPS-075",
   "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
   "FAM:SF-APPA-050"
  ],
  "summary": "Previous work (FAM:SF-APPS-075) has attempted to measure 'Extended Possession Value' including opponent T+1 suppression, finding small downstream effects for some play types (e.g., CUT, TRANSITION, SPOT_UP, POST_UP). However, these findings were noted as small and 'not a hypothesis test' due to unclear mechanisms. Another study (FAM:SF-CHT-029) explicitly tested for decaying cross-possession memory and found apparent effects to be artifacts of confounding. FAM:SF-APPA-050 also explored sequential suppression but noted issues with small sample sizes and lack of multiplicity correction. Critically, these attempts were often hampered or invalidated by underlying play-type label quality issues (FAM:SF-APPM-014, FAM:SF-PAP-096, FAM:SF-APPA-030, FAM:SF-CHT-025)."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
   "text": "The primary input (play-type labels) has demonstrated systematic inaccuracies, misclassifications, and inconsistencies across multiple audits and studies, directly impacting the validity of any PPP calculations and causal inferences."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "The sequential nature of basketball events introduces numerous confounders (e.g., score differential, time on clock, team strength, player fatigue, defensive scheme adjustments) that can falsely attribute lagged effects to specific play types."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "While the overall dataset is large, detecting statistically significant and practically meaningful small lagged effects for all permutations of play types and specific game situations, especially when considering 'AVOID' labels which imply rare negative outcomes, could lead to insufficient sample sizes for robust analysis."
  },
  {
   "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
   "text": "Ensuring that the 'AVOID' labels or the calculation of lagged PPP does not inadvertently incorporate future information or outcomes that are not truly available at the decision point of running a play."
  },
  {
   "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
   "text": "The definition of 'AVOID' labels itself could be problematic if it is too closely tied to the outcome being measured without independent criteria, leading to circular reasoning or overfitting."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.05,
  "rejected": 0.15,
  "untestable": 0.5,
  "artifact": 0.25,
  "uncertain": 0.05
 },
 "decision": "do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of running play type X on the opponent's Points Per Possession (PPP) in the immediate subsequent possession (lag-1), controlling for all observable confounding factors and ensuring robust play-type label quality.",
  "unit": "Basketball possession, defined as the sequence of events from a change of possession to the scoring or turnover event, with play types inferred per possession for both teams.",
  "sample": "All completed possessions from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs. Filter to possessions where a clear play type can be identified with high confidence, and where a subsequent opponent possession is completed.",
  "power": "Not estimable due to fundamental data quality issues. If data quality were resolved, power analysis would be conducted to detect a minimum effect size of 0.02 PPP with 80% power at alpha=0.05, considering the number of play types and average possessions per play type.",
  "controls": "Control for game state (score differential, time remaining, period), team and opponent strength (e.g., ELO rating, net rating), personnel on court (using lineup data), preceding possession outcome, and location (home/away). Consider a fixed-effects model for team and opponent to absorb unobserved heterogeneity. Propensity score matching or inverse probability weighting could be explored if appropriate for observed confounders.",
  "falsification": "A falsification test would involve assessing whether the lagged effect persists after randomizing play types within similar game states or if the effect collapses under within-team baselines, as observed in FAM:SF-CHT-029. Also, verify that the effect does not appear for 'impossible' lags (e.g., lag-100).",
  "multiplicity": "Given multiple play types and potential lags, apply a robust multiplicity correction (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg procedure or Bonferroni correction) to control the False Discovery Rate (FDR) across all tested hypotheses.",
  "decision_rule": "The research question would be considered 'supported' if a statistically significant (p < 0.05 after multiplicity correction) and practically meaningful (e.g., > 0.02 PPP change) lagged effect is observed for at least one major play type, consistently across seasons, and robust to confounding controls. The magnitude of this effect would then inform 'AVOID' labels. However, this is contingent on resolving the foundational label quality issues first.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 play-by-play, substitution data, and box scores. Crucially, a verified and audited version of the derived play-type possession records would be required, explicitly addressing and correcting the issues identified in FAM:SF-APPM-014, FAM:SF-PAP-096, FAM:SF-APPA-030, and FAM:SF-CHT-025."
 }
}

